    example: ['Let A = [[1, 2, 3], [2, 4, 6]], a 2 by 3 matrix.', 'Row 2 is twice row 1, so elimination leaves one pivot: rank(A) = 1. Every output is a multiple of [1, 2].', 'Solving x1 + 2x2 + 3x3 = 0 leaves x2 and x3 free, so nullity(A) = 2, with null space basis [-2,1,0] and [-3,0,1].', 'Check: rank + nullity = 1 + 2 = 3 = number of columns.'],
    graph: { type: 'determinant', title: 'Columns that line up lose a direction', caption: 'The two columns of a 2 by 2 matrix drawn as arrows. When they line up, the parallelogram flattens: rank drops to 1 and nullity rises to 1.', sliders: [{ key: 'a', label: 'a', min: -3, max: 3, step: 0.25, value: 2 }, { key: 'b', label: 'b', min: -3, max: 3, step: 0.25, value: 1 }, { key: 'c', label: 'c', min: -3, max: 3, step: 0.25, value: 1 }, { key: 'd', label: 'd', min: -3, max: 3, step: 0.25, value: 0.5 }] },
    figure: 'four-subspaces',
    misconception: 'Rank is not the number of nonzero rows or columns of the original matrix; it is the number of independent ones, found after elimination.',
